WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — rce-compile (45s) Narrator Lem

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:07.696
First, real compilers break any single descriptive law, so this study defines a seven-part yardstick instead.

2
00:00:08.136 --> 00:00:12.980
Second, maximum compiler optimization burns upfront energy that

3
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.901
never pays back on thirty percent of test programs.

4
00:00:17.341 --> 00:00:23.459
Third, fingerprinting code by variable position reuses builds with zero mix-ups,

5
00:00:23.459 --> 00:00:26.901
cutting Rust compile energy four hundredfold.

6
00:00:27.341 --> 00:00:34.656
Developers turn on maximum optimization out of habit. Yet spending tens of joules compiling a short-lived

7
00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:40.438
tool is like grinding an axe for hours to chop one twig—unless you reuse the blade.

8
00:00:40.878 --> 00:00:45.759
Pre-compiling shared runtime code once and indexing builds by variable

9
00:00:45.759 --> 00:00:51.825
binding positions slashes compilation energy while catching every swapped-variable bug.
